1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
So far we've covered the five principles.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
They're how you write any prompt well.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,571
Now let's add specific techniques —

4
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:12,000
patterns you'll reach for depending on the task.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:13,286
Eight in total.

6
00:00:13,286 --> 00:00:15,857
We'll cover seven in this session.

7
00:00:15,857 --> 00:00:18,429
The eighth — comparative prompting —

8
00:00:18,429 --> 00:00:22,714
fits better with the advanced patterns in the next session,

9
00:00:22,714 --> 00:00:24,429
so I'll save it.

10
00:00:24,429 --> 00:00:25,629
Quick map.

11
00:00:25,629 --> 00:00:26,829
Zero-shot.

12
00:00:26,829 --> 00:00:28,029
Few-shot.

13
00:00:28,029 --> 00:00:29,229
Chain-of-thought.

14
00:00:29,229 --> 00:00:30,429
Role prompting.

15
00:00:30,429 --> 00:00:31,629
Iterative refinement.

16
00:00:31,629 --> 00:00:32,829
Template prompting.

17
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:34,029
Negative prompting.

18
00:00:34,029 --> 00:00:36,171
And in Session 4, comparative.

19
00:00:36,171 --> 00:00:39,171
Don't worry about memorizing all eight names.

20
00:00:39,171 --> 00:00:43,886
The names matter less than knowing when to reach for which

21
00:00:43,886 --> 00:00:45,086
one.

22
00:00:45,086 --> 00:00:47,657
By the end of the workbook,

23
00:00:47,657 --> 00:00:50,657
you'll have used most of them naturally.

24
00:00:50,657 --> 00:00:55,371
Let's start with the two most common — zero-shot and few-shot.

25
00:00:55,371 --> 00:00:58,800
Zero-shot is what you've probably been doing already.

26
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:02,229
You ask the AI to do a task,

27
00:01:02,229 --> 00:01:04,371
without showing it any examples.

28
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:07,800
"Translate this English text to French." That's zero-shot.

29
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:12,086
The AI relies entirely on what it learned during training.

30
00:01:12,086 --> 00:01:14,657
It works for simple, common tasks.

31
00:01:14,657 --> 00:01:15,857
Translation.

32
00:01:15,857 --> 00:01:17,057
Summarization.

33
00:01:17,057 --> 00:01:18,257
Quick questions.

34
00:01:18,257 --> 00:01:22,971
The trade-off is that you have less control over format and

35
00:01:22,971 --> 00:01:27,257
style — and for unusual tasks, results can be inconsistent.

36
00:01:27,257 --> 00:01:31,971
Few-shot is when you give the AI two or three examples

37
00:01:31,971 --> 00:01:33,686
before the actual task.

38
00:01:33,686 --> 00:01:37,971
And honestly — it's the highest-payoff technique you can learn.

39
00:01:37,971 --> 00:01:39,171
Watch this.

40
00:01:39,171 --> 00:01:43,886
I want the AI to convert customer reviews into a number

41
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:45,600
from 1 to 5.

42
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:48,600
So I show it three examples first.

43
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,029
"Review: 'This product exceeded my expectations.' Sentiment: 5.

44
00:01:52,029 --> 00:01:55,029
Review: 'It's okay, nothing special.' Sentiment: 3.

45
00:01:55,029 --> 00:01:56,314
Review: 'Terrible quality,

46
00:01:56,314 --> 00:02:01,029
broke after one use.' Sentiment: 1." And then I add the

47
00:02:01,029 --> 00:02:03,600
new review I actually want scored.

48
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,743
The AI follows the pattern.

49
00:02:05,743 --> 00:02:09,171
It doesn't just understand what I want —

50
00:02:09,171 --> 00:02:11,314
it sees what I want.

51
00:02:11,314 --> 00:02:16,029
Few-shot is your tool when you need consistent format, custom style,

52
00:02:16,029 --> 00:02:17,314
or domain-specific output.

53
00:02:17,314 --> 00:02:20,314
Two or three examples is usually enough.

54
00:02:20,314 --> 00:02:22,029
You don't need ten.

55
00:02:22,029 --> 00:02:26,743
And one tip — make sure your examples are consistently labeled.

56
00:02:26,743 --> 00:02:31,029
If your "5" examples and your "4" examples sound similar,

57
00:02:31,029 --> 00:02:33,171
the model will get confused.

58
00:02:33,171 --> 00:02:34,371
Be deliberate.

59
00:02:34,371 --> 00:02:36,086
Technique three — chain-of-thought.

60
00:02:36,086 --> 00:02:37,800
This one's almost magical.

61
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:40,800
Ask the AI a multi-step reasoning problem,

62
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,086
and add the words "Let's think step by step" —

63
00:02:45,086 --> 00:02:47,229
and the accuracy goes up.

64
00:02:47,229 --> 00:02:48,429
Sometimes dramatically.

65
00:02:48,429 --> 00:02:50,143
Here's a math problem.

66
00:02:50,143 --> 00:02:52,714
"If a store has 15 apples,

67
00:02:52,714 --> 00:02:55,286
and sells 40 percent of them,

68
00:02:55,286 --> 00:02:59,143
then receives a shipment of 8 more apples —

69
00:02:59,143 --> 00:03:02,571
how many apples does it have?" Without chain-of-thought,

70
00:03:02,571 --> 00:03:04,714
the AI might just guess.

71
00:03:04,714 --> 00:03:07,714
With chain-of-thought, it works through the steps.

72
00:03:07,714 --> 00:03:09,000
Starting count, fifteen.

73
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,429
Sold, forty percent of fifteen — that's six.

74
00:03:12,429 --> 00:03:13,629
Remaining, nine.

75
00:03:13,629 --> 00:03:14,914
New shipment, eight.

76
00:03:14,914 --> 00:03:16,200
Total — seventeen.

77
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:18,771
The other thing chain-of-thought does —

78
00:03:18,771 --> 00:03:21,771
and this is just as valuable —

79
00:03:21,771 --> 00:03:24,343
it lets you check the work.

80
00:03:24,343 --> 00:03:28,200
If the AI made a mistake at step three,

81
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:29,914
you can see it.

82
00:03:29,914 --> 00:03:33,343
You don't have to trust an opaque answer.

83
00:03:33,343 --> 00:03:35,914
Use it for math, multi-step decisions,

84
00:03:35,914 --> 00:03:39,343
anything where you'd want to verify the logic.

85
00:03:39,343 --> 00:03:43,200
One thing to note — for genuinely simple tasks,

86
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:44,486
chain-of-thought adds noise.

87
00:03:44,486 --> 00:03:49,200
Don't reach for it on a translation, or a one-line summary.

88
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:52,200
Save it for when the reasoning matters.

89
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:54,343
Technique four — role prompting.

90
00:03:54,343 --> 00:03:59,057
You can ask the AI to take on a specific persona,

91
00:03:59,057 --> 00:04:01,629
and the response shape will shift.

92
00:04:01,629 --> 00:04:06,343
"You are a blockchain expert with ten years of experience explaining

93
00:04:06,343 --> 00:04:11,057
complex concepts to business executives — explain blockchain, highlighting business value,

94
00:04:11,057 --> 00:04:14,486
avoiding jargon." Compare that to just "explain blockchain".

95
00:04:14,486 --> 00:04:19,200
The role version comes back focused on outcomes, ROI, real-world deployment.

96
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:22,629
The basic version comes back with cryptographic primitives.

97
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:27,343
Roles work because they activate the patterns the model has seen

98
00:04:27,343 --> 00:04:28,543
during training.

99
00:04:28,543 --> 00:04:32,400
Naming a role isn't magic — it's pattern matching.

100
00:04:32,400 --> 00:04:33,686
So be specific.

101
00:04:33,686 --> 00:04:36,686
"You are an expert" is too vague.

102
00:04:36,686 --> 00:04:41,400
"You are a senior pediatric nurse explaining a procedure to a

103
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:45,257
worried parent" — that's a role that does work.

104
00:04:45,257 --> 00:04:47,400
Technique five — iterative refinement.

105
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:52,114
This one's so simple it almost doesn't sound like a technique.

106
00:04:52,114 --> 00:04:56,400
After the AI gives you a response, don't start over.

107
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:57,686
Build on it.

108
00:04:57,686 --> 00:04:59,400
"Make it more playful,

109
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:04,114
targeting millennials." "Tighten the second paragraph." "Now give me three variations

110
00:05:04,114 --> 00:05:07,971
with different opening lines." Iteration is faster than rewriting.

111
00:05:07,971 --> 00:05:10,114
It builds on what's working.

112
00:05:10,114 --> 00:05:14,829
And it's how you'll do most of your real work —

113
00:05:14,829 --> 00:05:19,543
the first draft is a starting point, not a finish line.

114
00:05:19,543 --> 00:05:23,829
The discipline is in being specific about what to change.

115
00:05:23,829 --> 00:05:26,400
"Make it better" is wasted breath.

116
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,686
"Cut the introduction and start with the second paragraph" —

117
00:05:30,686 --> 00:05:33,257
that the AI can act on.

118
00:05:33,257 --> 00:05:35,829
Two more techniques in this section.

119
00:05:35,829 --> 00:05:37,971
Technique six — template prompting.

120
00:05:37,971 --> 00:05:42,257
A template is just a structured prompt format you reuse.

121
00:05:42,257 --> 00:05:43,457
Task.

122
00:05:43,457 --> 00:05:44,657
Context.

123
00:05:44,657 --> 00:05:45,857
Format.

124
00:05:45,857 --> 00:05:47,057
Constraints.

125
00:05:47,057 --> 00:05:48,257
Example.

126
00:05:48,257 --> 00:05:51,257
You fill in the blanks each time.

127
00:05:51,257 --> 00:05:55,543
The benefit isn't that templates make individual prompts better —

128
00:05:55,543 --> 00:05:58,543
it's that they make your team consistent.

129
00:05:58,543 --> 00:06:03,257
If five people on your team use the same template for

130
00:06:03,257 --> 00:06:04,457
product descriptions,

131
00:06:04,457 --> 00:06:09,171
you'll get five outputs that sound like they came from one

132
00:06:09,171 --> 00:06:10,371
brand.

133
00:06:10,371 --> 00:06:12,086
That's worth a lot.

134
00:06:12,086 --> 00:06:16,800
Build a template for any task you do more than three

135
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,000
times.

136
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,429
The first version doesn't have to be perfect.

137
00:06:21,429 --> 00:06:24,000
It just has to be reusable.

138
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,143
Technique seven — negative prompting.

139
00:06:26,143 --> 00:06:30,857
Negative prompting is when you tell the AI what not to

140
00:06:30,857 --> 00:06:32,057
do.

141
00:06:32,057 --> 00:06:33,771
"Write a professional email.

142
00:06:33,771 --> 00:06:36,343
Do not use overly formal language.

143
00:06:36,343 --> 00:06:38,486
Do not include unnecessary apologies.

144
00:06:38,486 --> 00:06:41,486
Do not make promises we can't keep.

145
00:06:41,486 --> 00:06:46,200
Do not exceed 150 words." It works because sometimes it's easier

146
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:50,486
to say what you don't want than what you do.

147
00:06:50,486 --> 00:06:55,200
And when you've been frustrated by the same kind of bad

148
00:06:55,200 --> 00:06:56,914
output a few times,

149
00:06:56,914 --> 00:07:01,629
listing the failure modes upfront tells the AI exactly what to

150
00:07:01,629 --> 00:07:02,829
avoid.

151
00:07:02,829 --> 00:07:04,971
The trick is being specific.

152
00:07:04,971 --> 00:07:07,114
"Don't be boring" doesn't work.

153
00:07:07,114 --> 00:07:11,400
"Don't use the word 'leverage' or 'synergy'" — that works.

154
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:12,600
That's seven.

155
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:16,886
In the next session, we'll wrap up the eighth —

156
00:07:16,886 --> 00:07:21,600
comparative prompting — and stack on four advanced patterns: system prompts,

157
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:24,171
temperature, prompt chaining, and meta prompting.

158
00:07:24,171 --> 00:07:27,600
If you want to try few-shot right now,

159
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:31,457
open Workbook Task 2 — the support ticket triager.

160
00:07:31,457 --> 00:07:34,457
It's the highest-reward exercise in the workbook.

161
00:07:34,457 --> 00:07:37,029
Try it before moving on. ---
